  static const String _privacyPolicyBody =
      "OWNER APPROVED 2026-08-31; COUNSEL REVIEW PENDING. This marking does not state or imply that counsel reviewed this policy. Privacy Policy. Win's VPN processes only the data needed to provide and protect the service. Before you accept this policy, the app uses only protected local settings needed to remember your choice. It does not read a device hardware identifier, request coarse public-IP location or map tiles, fetch VPN nodes or other network data, run tunnel or speed-test smoke checks, or request battery-optimization changes. If you choose Not now, those operations remain off and you can reopen this bundled policy from the app.\n\nData processed after acceptance. For activation and the three-device limit, the app generates a random app-install identifier on the device on first run and sends it as a Device or other ID together with the device model, platform, app version, locale, selected location, capability flags, and the account number or free-access identity. The identifier is created locally and is not derived from a device hardware identifier or an advertising identifier. On Android, Windows, and Linux it is kept in the app's private storage and reinstalling the app produces a new one; on Apple platforms it is kept in the device keychain and can survive a reinstall on the same device. The identifier may be linked to the account for service functionality, account management, fraud prevention, security, abuse prevention, and device-limit enforcement. It is not used for ads or tracking, sold, or shared for advertising. Selected split-tunneling package identifiers remain in protected local settings and are passed locally to the operating-system VPN service; the full installed-app inventory is not uploaded. The app also generates a long-lived cryptographic key pair on first use of each account and derives a public key from it; that public key is transmitted to Win's VPN with every account verification and configuration renewal request, not only at activation, and acts as one of the persistent identifiers identifying this device to the service for connection provisioning, the three-device limit, and abuse prevention. It is a long-lived linkable identifier that stays the same across app relaunches and reconnects, functions as a repeatable device marker, and is not derived from hardware or advertising identifiers. It is stored in the app's private storage or the platform keychain, and the account-side record is deleted or overwritten when the device is deactivated, replaced, or no longer needed.\n\nNetwork and approximate location. After acceptance, the app may contact Win's VPN gateways to obtain nodes, account status, announcements, tunnel configuration, and security updates. So that the app can still reach a working gateway when the usual DNS or CDN paths are censored, it also requests a small gateway-recovery file hosted on GitHub at raw.githubusercontent.com. GitHub necessarily receives the connection IP and ordinary request metadata for that request. That file only selects among gateways already built into the signed app, and no account number, device identifier, or other user data is sent with the request. Only after you ask to show the approximate-location map, before a VPN connection, the app asks first-party Win's VPN origins and gateways for an IP-derived map scene and uses Cloudflare's speed.cloudflare.com/meta as the final fallback. Those services receive the pre-VPN connection IP and ordinary request metadata. Apple Maps receives the requested region and connection IP on Apple platforms; on other platforms a first-party Win's VPN endpoint serves OpenStreetMap-derived tiles. Win's VPN does not request device GPS for this feature or retain the returned public IP, city, postal code, or map-view history. When the user runs the in-app speed test, the app measures throughput against third-party test servers: speed.cloudflare.com and cachefly.cachefly.net for every region, and, for nodes in the western United States, losangeles.ca.speedtest.frontier.com, lsanca-speedtest-ookla-02.st.charter.com, and speedtest.lax2.sonic.net. Each server receives the connection IP, ordinary request metadata, and the transferred measurement traffic; no account number, device identifier, or other app data is included, and these requests occur only while the user is running the speed test.\n\nPayments, support, and providers. Account email is processed when supplied for a paid/manual account or support. PayPal processes payment details and sends Win's VPN payment confirmation metadata; Win's VPN does not receive or store raw card data. SMTP2GO processes transactional email. Hosting, CDN, security, app-store, and platform providers process the minimum data needed for their functions. If an account is created through a referral link, the referring account is recorded against the referred account so that the account-validity bonus can be granted and reversed if the payment is refunded or cancelled. Support messages are processed only when the user supplies them. A distribution platform may provide aggregated crash or diagnostic information under its own controls; the app has no crash or analytics SDK and does not upload crash reports. Website analytics or ad-conversion events are processed only with website consent and are separate from VPN tunnel traffic. Data may be disclosed to authorities only when required by valid legal process.\n\nAbuse prevention and rate limiting. To throttle repeated failed attempts and protect accounts, gateways, and infrastructure, the service keeps short-lived request counters and security-event records keyed to a hashed form of the source IP address, device identifier, or account number rather than to the address itself. These records exist only for security, fraud prevention, abuse response, and device-limit enforcement. They are not used to profile a user, are not used for advertising, and never contain VPN traffic, browsing history, DNS queries, or destination activity.\n\nTrusted contact and duress response. Win's VPN does not ship this feature. There is no duress code, no duress wipe, and no trusted-contact alert on any platform, including Android and iOS. No duress credential can be enrolled, no trusted-contact phone number or email is requested or stored, the Android release build declares no SMS permission and sends no SMS, and the app sends no contact, coordinate, or alert to Win's VPN servers or to any third party.\n\nNo VPN activity logs. Win's VPN does not collect or keep browsing history, DNS query history, traffic contents, raw VPN traffic, or destination activity and does not sell personal or sensitive data. Free-access verification and sign-out create a 30-day event containing the shared free identity, device and source-IP buckets, device public-key fingerprint, platform and device model, selected gateway, stealth choice, a user-agent hash, and time. It records that a free connection happened and which gateway served it, never what was done through it; paid accounts do not generate these events. Transient connection/session state is cleared when the VPN disconnects. Device-limit metadata is deleted or overwritten when a device is deactivated, replaced, or no longer needed. App-collected operational data no longer needed is deleted or anonymized within 90 days, except while required for active-account operation, payment or accounting, legal acceptance, security, fraud prevention, abuse response, or dispute resolution. Request access, correction, deletion, or privacy help at privacy@winsvpn.org or support@winsvpn.org. Where applicable law provides additional portability, restriction, objection, appeal, or regulator-complaint rights, request them at privacy@winsvpn.org. Jurisdiction-specific legal bases, transfer mechanisms, and representative duties remain counsel-pending. \"Verified No Log\" is Win's VPN's name for these commitments and has not been independently audited or attested as of this version. English is the controlling version; translations are for convenience.";
  static const String _legalFallbackBody =
