necessarily receives the connection IP and ordinary request metadata for that request, the file only selects among gateways already built into the signed app, and no account number, device identifier, or other user data is sent with it. To throttle repeated failed attempts and protect accounts, gateways, and infrastructure, the service keeps short-lived request counters and security-event records keyed to a hashed form of the source IP address, device identifier, or account number rather than to the address itself; those records exist only for security, fraud prevention, abuse response, and device-limit enforcement and never contain VPN traffic, browsing history, DNS queries, or destination activity. Only after the user asks to show the approximate-location map, before the VPN connects, the app asks first-party Win's VPN origins and gateways for an IP-derived map scene and uses Cloudflare's speed.cloudflare.com/meta as the final fallback. Those services receive the pre-VPN connection IP. Apple Maps receives the region and connection IP on Apple platforms; a first-party Win's VPN endpoint serves OpenStreetMap-derived tiles on other platforms; Win's VPN does not request device GPS for this feature or retain the returned IP, city, postal code, or map-view history. The app also generates a long-lived cryptographic key pair on first use of each account and derives a public key from it, transmitting that public key to Win's VPN with every account verification and configuration renewal request as a long-lived linkable device identifier for connection provisioning, the three-device limit, and abuse prevention; it is not derived from hardware or advertising identifiers and the account-side record is deleted or overwritten when the device is deactivated, replaced, or no longer needed. When the user runs the in-app speed test, the app measures throughput against speed.cloudflare.com and cachefly.cachefly.net for every region, and, for nodes in the western United States, losangeles.ca.speedtest.frontier.com, lsanca-speedtest-ookla-02.st.charter.com, and speedtest.lax2.sonic.net; each receives the connection IP, ordinary request metadata, and the transferred measurement traffic, with no account number, device identifier, or other app data included, and only while the test is running. Transient connection/session state is cleared when the VPN disconnects. Device-limit metadata is deleted or overwritten when the device is deactivated, replaced, or no longer needed for active account operation, and app-collected operational data that is no longer needed is deleted or anonymized within 90 days, except where longer retention is required for payment, legal, security, fraud-prevention, dispute-resolution, or active-account operation. Win Empire LLC maintains internal IP/open-source license compliance, written quality control, release testing, change management, customer acceptance, and company signoff procedures. Android and iOS launcher icon camouflage and Android/iOS in-app calculator camouflage are user-safety features for high-risk censorship environments, including Burma/Myanmar, where a visible VPN interface can expose users to retaliation. On Android and iOS both are applied automatically once a device is activated and cannot be switched off in the app; the real interface is shown after the PIN is entered. Android TV and desktop builds have neither. They do not hide malicious activity, iOS may display an operating-system confirmation when changing the icon, and the operating system's VPN status remains visible while a tunnel is active. Trusted contact and duress response: Win's VPN does not ship this feature. There is no duress code, no duress wipe, and no trusted-contact alert on any platform, including Android and iOS. No duress credential can be enrolled, no trusted-contact phone number or email is requested or stored, the Android release build declares no SMS permission and sends no SMS, and the app sends no contact, coordinate, or alert to Win's VPN servers or to any third party. Win's VPN includes third-party open-source components governed by their own licenses and notices; those upstream projects are separate from Win's VPN Inc. and Win Empire LLC and do not sponsor or endorse Win's VPN. Win's VPN Inc. and Win Empire LLC maintain insurance-readiness controls and may seek cyber or Technology Errors and Omissions coverage. No particular policy is represented as bound or in force. Coverage must be confirmed from a current binder or issued policy. If coverage exists, it creates no service warranty, direct claim right, third-party beneficiary status, guaranteed recovery, or higher liability cap for a customer or third party. The service is provided without a guarantee that every network, country, website, app, streaming service, device, or censorship system will be reachable at all times. These terms are governed by Florida law except where non-waivable consumer laws require otherwise. English is the controlling legal version; translations are convenience-only.";
